  "text": "Since $P$, $Q$, and $R$ are the midpoints of sides $AB$, $BC$, and $CA$ respectively, the three circles with diameters $AP$, $BQ$, and $CR$ intersect at corresponding points $X$, $Y$, and $Z$.\n\nLet's denote the lengths of the segments connecting the midpoints to the vertices of the triangle as follows:\n\n$AP = y$\n\n$BQ = z$\n\n$CR = b$\n\nBy the properties of midpoints and the lengths of the sides of the triangle, we have:\n\n$AP = \\frac{AB}{2} = \\frac{13}{2}$\n\n$BQ = \\frac{BC}{2} = \\frac{14}{2} = 7$\n\n$CR = \\frac{CA}{2} = \\frac{15}{2}$\n\nSince the circles intersect at corresponding points, the triangles formed by these points and the midpoints will have equal areas to the original triangle $ABC$.\n\nSo, the area of each of three triangles will be $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot (AP \\cdot BQ \\cdot CR)$\n\n$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot (\\frac{13}{2} \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\frac{15}{2})$\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n$\\frac{13}{2} \\cdot 49 \\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{8775 \\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{16}$\n\nHence, the area of $\\triangle XYZ = \\frac{8775 \\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{16}$.",
